WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.740
Put healthy volunteers in dim light, on a scheduled 28-hour day.

2
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.580
Their internal day averaged 24.18 hours.

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.280
That is a study mean, and individuals vary.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.740
So what brings the clock back to 24, every day?

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:24.493
In mouse cells, a pair of proteins, CLOCK and BMAL1, binds short DNA sequences

6
00:00:24.493 --> 00:00:28.113
called E-boxes. That switches on the Per and Cry genes.

7
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:32.813
PER and CRY proteins build up, bind each other, and enter the nucleus.

8
00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:39.613
In reporter assays, either CRY1 or CRY2 abolished CLOCK-BMAL1-driven transcription.

9
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:44.657
As PER and CRY are degraded, the brake comes off, and a new cycle begins.

10
00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:47.017
Why does this oscillate, and not settle?

11
00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:49.017
Think of a shower with a long pipe.

12
00:00:49.597 --> 00:00:53.197
You turn toward hot, nothing changes, and when the hot water arrives, you have

13
00:00:53.197 --> 00:00:57.457
overshot. With enough delay and steep repression, negative feedback keeps

14
00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:58.877
overshooting in both directions.

15
00:00:59.697 --> 00:01:04.997
Here, transcription, translation, modification, nuclear entry and timed degradation

16
00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:06.497
supply hours of delay.

17
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.610
The loop runs near 24 hours, not exactly.

18
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.790
Take an idealized clock with a period of 24.18 hours.

19
00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.330
It has no time cues, and starts in phase with the day.

20
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.970
Each cycle runs 0.18 hours over, which is 10.8 minutes.

21
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:28.910
After seven cycles, its marker falls 75.6 minutes later than on the day.

22
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:29.910
clock.

23
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:35.280
Light must reset the clock, and it reaches the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the SCN,

24
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.140
along the retinohypothalamic tract.

25
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.400
A tract is a bundle of axons.

26
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.320
In isolated rat retina, ganglion cells that project there still depolarized to

27
00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.600
light, with rod and cone input blocked.

28
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.680
They express melanopsin, and rods and cones contribute as well.

29
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:56.320
In the mouse SCN, light pulses strong enough to reset the clock quickly induced Per1

30
00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.320
transcripts.

31
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:01.773
In healthy people under controlled conditions, bright light before the

32
00:02:01.773 --> 00:02:04.013
core-body-temperature minimum delayed the clock.

33
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:07.513
That minimum usually falls in the early morning hours.

34
00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:09.433
Light after it advanced the clock.

35
00:02:09.973 --> 00:02:15.113
To stay at 24 hours, this clock needs an average net advance of 10.8 minutes each

36
00:02:15.113 --> 00:02:19.873
day. Keep this: the clock runs near a day, and light resets it daily.
